  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="commodity_brassel_cb194134d28053f0f82027b25e07fd1cc653dab1e0f864d7ae58d110a1c48a2443c">
    <crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>the hard brownish-red wood of an East Indian tree, known as Sappan (Cæsalpinia Sappan), from which dyers obtain a red color (OED s.v. Brazil1 I 1) | Wood of the species Caesalpinia sappan, native to Sri Lanka, India, and Malaya. [Source: Getty AAT] | Red dye produced from the hot water extraction of any of several tropical trees of the Caesalpinia genus. Its principal colorant is brasilin, a hydroxyanthraquinone. Used to dye textiles as early as the 12th century in Europe; the color is not lightfast and fades when heated. [Source: Getty AAT]</crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>
    <crm:P2_has_type rdf:resource="http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object"/>
  </rdf:Description>
